    desc: "Push Pin was founded in New York in 1954 by Cooper Union graduates Seymour Chwast, Milton Glaser, Reynold Ruffins and Edward Sorel, with the image-and-text Push Pin Almanack as calling card. From 1957 the studio periodical the Push Pin Graphic carried the look worldwide for over two decades. The style deliberately raided art history — Victorian ornaments, Art Nouveau curves, Expressionist woodcut, comic-cartoon line — rejecting both Swiss purism and bland magazine naturalism in favor of illustration-driven design. Glaser's 1967 Bob Dylan silhouette poster and Chwast's 1968 End Bad Breath antiwar poster became defining images of the era. Its historicist eclecticism shaped 1960s–70s American editorial illustration and anticipated postmodern graphic design's historical sampling. Critics later charged the approach with stylistic recycling, but its wit and drawing-first craft remain a studio reference point.",
