    desc: "Grunge typography names the distressed, anti-grid editorial style David Carson developed through surf and skate magazines and then Ray Gun, the alternative-rock monthly he art-directed from 1992 to 1995. Columns run sideways or overlap, headlines stretch and knock out, body text sits at low legibility as pure texture, and ink-scuffed photocopied surfaces replace clean paper. Its most quoted gesture ran a Bryan Ferry interview set entirely in dingbats; readers complained, and Carson argued you do not have to read it to feel it. The style borrowed from punk collage and skate zines, then fed directly into 1990s advertising and music-industry design. Lewis Blackwell and Carson's book The End of Print (1995) codified the debate, which pitched expressive mess against Swiss-modern legibility. Long after Ray Gun, the manner survives wherever music and youth culture want an anti-corporate print texture.",
