    desc: "Deconstructive typography adapted post-structuralist theory to graphic design through two centers. At Cranbrook Academy of Art in the 1970s–80s, Katherine McCoy's program encouraged students to layer, fragment and collide type and image so communication itself became the subject. In California, Rudy VanderLans and Zuzana Licko's Emigre magazine (1984–2005) and Emigre Fonts gave the discourse a publishing platform and a catalogue of deliberately difficult typefaces, among them Licko's bitmap-based Emperor, Oblong and Matrix. Jeffery Keedy's Keedy Sans (1993) became a signature of the tendency, and his essays defended difficulty as content. Unlike David Carson's intuitive mess, this current argued from Derrida and design pedagogy, treating margins, baselines and reading order as conventions to expose. Its polemics shaped 1990s type design, design writing and theory-heavy graduate programs.",
