    sum: "State agitation printing that fused bold diagonals, simple slogans and heroic silhouettes into one of design history's most copied languages.",
    desc: "After the October Revolution the new state needed to address a largely illiterate public, and the poster became its primary instrument. ROSTA Windows (1919–1921) used stencilled cartoon news-sheets pasted in shop windows, while Dmitry Moor's Have You Enlisted as a Volunteer? (1920) fixed the template of a pointing figure, flat red and black, and a blunt slogan. The 1920s added constructivist photomontage by Gustav Klutsis and the Stenberg brothers' dynamic film posters, before policy steered output toward socialist realism's polished heroic manner. Wartime images such as Irakly Toidze's The Motherland Calls! (1941) carried the tradition to its widest audience. The grammar — two-color contrast, thrusting diagonals, wedge compositions, shortened imperative text — was built for reading at speed and distance, and it remains one of the most referenced bodies of political graphic design.",
