    sum: "Cheap giant letterpress show posters whose stacked ornamental wood type and loud chromatic inks became the 19th century's visual noise.",
    desc: "Wood type made poster printing cheap at architectural scale: cut in relief from end-grain blocks from the late 1820s and paired with rapid cylinder presses, it let jobbing printers compose pages of huge condensed, fat-face and ornamented letters. Victorian circuses, medicine shows, Wild West exhibitions and railroads ordered broadsides whose typography was stacked, arced, inlined and shaded to fill every inch, printed in bright chrome yellows, vermilion and Prussian blues. Firms like Hamilton in Two Rivers, Wisconsin industrialized the trade, and Hatch Show Print in Nashville (from 1879) still works the same way. Composers treated letterforms as collage units decades before modernism, which made the form a favorite citation of late-twentieth-century designers. Rob Roy Kelly's collected American wood-type specimens remain the standard reference for the faces.",
