    sum: "Freehand single-stroke enamel linework — scrolls, scallops and eyeballs — brushed onto hot rods as signatures of kustom kulture.",
    desc: "Pinstriping decorates vehicles with impossibly thin parallel lines, scallops and scrolling script pulled in one wet stroke with sword-shaped brushes. Its hot-rod flowering came in postwar Southern California, where Kenneth Howard — Von Dutch — turned shop craft into a personal art, signing hoods and fuel tanks with his flying-eyeball emblem. The practice descends from carriage and sign-painting striping and runs alongside Ed Big Daddy Roth's monster-shirt empire within kustom kulture, the custom-car scene's whole visual culture. Stripers work freehand with one-shot enamel that levels itself, so the finished line records every speed change of the hand. After fading with 1960s customs, the craft revived through 1990s rockabilly and lowbrow art shows, and stripers now demonstrate at events like the Grand National Roadster Show. Motorcycle tanks, flight jackets and bicycles carry the same line vocabulary.",
