    cat: "Graphic Design", c: "airbrushed custom-vehicle vernacular art", e: "commercial_style", o: "1970s American custom-van boom (vanning), tied to surf, custom-car and airbrush trade shops; strongest in California",
    g: "United States, with echoes in Australia and Europe", p: "c. 1972–1982 peak; revival imagery since", era: "1970s leisure and custom-car culture",
    sum: "Airbrushed murals of wizards, sunsets and chrome that turned 1970s custom vans into rolling fantasy billboards.",
    desc: "During the 1970s vanning boom, owners of customized Chevrolet, Ford and Dodge vans treated large side panels as canvas, commissioning airbrushed murals of wizards, dragons, eagles, fantasy women and sunset skies. The airbrush's soft, seamless gradients made it the tool of custom-paint shops, boardwalk T-shirt stands and biker publishing alike; David Mann's Easyriders paintings spread the same visual language. Interiors matched the paint with shag carpet, porthole windows and captain's chairs, and van nationals awarded trophies for the most extravagant conversions. Custom-van magazines documented the scene before it collapsed around 1982, after which the imagery froze into a nostalgia shorthand for the decade. The craft flows through lowrider murals and T-shirt art, and contemporary painters revive the style for its unapologetic gradient richness.",
