    g: "United States, centered in San Francisco; UK and European echoes", p: "c. 1968–1977 peak", era: "hippie counterculture print era",
    sum: "Adult countercultural comic books sold through head shops, whose raw ink craft and taboo subjects split comix from the Comics Code.",
    desc: "Underground comix emerged from the San Francisco counterculture when Robert Crumb's Zap Comix #1 (1968) proved a market for explicitly adult comic books, deliberately spelled comix to mark the difference. Distributed through head shops, campus bookstores and street vendors, they bypassed newsstand censorship and the Comics Code with frank sex, drugs and political rage. The house craft was dense hand-inked line — crosshatch, stipple, wobbly pen weight — printed on cheap newsprint in thick multi-artist anthologies. Crumb's Fritz the Cat and Mr. Natural, Gilbert Shelton's Fabulous Furry Freak Brothers and the women's anthology Wimmen's Comix (1972) defined its range, and Aline Kominsky-Crumb's confessional rawness opened autobiographical comics. Police raids, rising costs and the decline of head shops ended the boom by the late 1970s, but the comix generation seeded alternative graphic novels and Raw magazine.",
