    cat: "Illustration & Comics", c: "Japanese girls'-magazine manga visual language", e: "commercial_style", o: "Japanese monthly shōjo magazines (Nakayoshi, Margaret, Ribbon) from the 1950s; revolutionized by the Year 24 Group in the 1970s",
    g: "Japan; global through translated manga and anime", p: "c. 1953–present; 1970s as style-defining decade", era: "postwar Japanese magazine culture",
    sum: "Japanese girls'-magazine manga built from starry eyes, floral panels and emotional space, revolutionized by the Year 24 Group.",
    desc: "Shojo manga grew in monthly girls' magazines such as Nakayoshi, Margaret and Ribbon after Osamu Tezuka's Princess Knight (1953) established big-eyed, theatrical drawing for young female readers. The 1970s Year 24 Group — Moto Hagio, Keiko Takemiya, Riyoko Ikeda and peers — blew the form open with psychological interiority, gender-fluid heroes and balletic panel flow; Ikeda's The Rose of Versailles became a generational monument. The visual language keeps expanding: enormous luminous eyes, backgrounds dissolving into flowers, sparkles and light blooms, panels that fragment to render feeling rather than space, and dense screentone gradients. CLAMP's ornate 1990s style carried it to global audiences through Cardcaptor Sakura. The aesthetic shapes not just manga but anime adaptations and fandom fashion, remaining one of the most imitated drawing systems in the world.",
    col: [{ h: "#f2a5bd", n: "rose pink" }, { h: "#d9c7ee", n: "pale lilac" }, { h: "#fff7f9", n: "sparkle white" }, { h: "#bfe6d2", n: "mint accent" }, { h: "#5c3a58", n: "deep plum" }],
