    cat: "Illustration & Comics", c: "cheap-magazine commercial cover painting", e: "historical", o: "US pulp-paper magazine industry from Argosy (1896); specialist cover painters working for New York publishers",
    g: "United States; British and Canadian reprints", p: "c. 1896–1955", era: "pulp magazine golden age",
    sum: "Lurid fast-paint covers that sold rough-paper fiction magazines on newsstands and shaped a century of genre imagery.",
    desc: "Pulp magazines — cheap wood-pulp fiction monthlies running from Argosy (1896) into the 1950s — competed on newsstands almost entirely through cover art. Painters working in days for modest fees delivered literal, high-velocity scenes: monsters looming over heroines, ray guns, hard fists and jungle horizons, always keyed to the title's promise. Frank R. Paul's covers for Amazing Stories (from 1926) effectively designed science fiction's visual vocabulary; Margaret Brundage's Weird Tales covers (1932–38) gave sword-and-sorcery its menace and made her the era's most talked-about cover artist; H.J. Ward's Doc Savage cover (1933) remains iconic. Virgil Finlay's scratchboard interiors pushed the craft further inside. The grammar — saturated primaries, dramatic diagonals, poster-scale mastheads — fed directly into comic books, paperback covers and film posters, and pulps are now collected as American vernacular masterpieces.",
    col: [{ h: "#c43e2a", n: "danger red" }, { h: "#e0a33c", n: "lurid yellow" }, { h: "#2b3f6b", n: "midnight blue" }, { h: "#f2e3c2", n: "aged paper" }, { h: "#3a2a20", n: "wood-pulp brown" }],
