    g: "Belgium, France, francophone Switzerland; global readership", p: "1929–present; album format codified 1940s–60s", era: "European postwar comic culture",
    sum: "The album-format European comic tradition — from Hergé's clear line to Franquin's elastic comedy and Moebius's cosmic vistas.",
    desc: "Franco-Belgian bandes dessinées grew from Catholic Belgian and French children's weeklies: Hergé's Tintin debuted in Le Petit Vingtième in 1929, Spirou magazine followed in 1938, and the postwar Tintin and Spirou schools codified two graphic temperaments — Hergé's flat-color clarity of line and André Franquin's rubbery comic energy. The 48-page hardcover album, color-separated in the same flat palette system as animation, became the format's signature object. Pilote (1959, René Goscinny and Albert Uderzo) added a more adult, satirical French register, home of Asterix. From the 1970s the magazine Métal hurlant and artists like Moebius (Jean Giraud) pushed the tradition toward science-fiction dreamscape. Bande dessinée is institutionalized as the ninth art, with a national vocabulary of album shops, the Angoulême festival, and Brussels's Comic Strip Route murals.",
    col: [{ h: "#e0492f", n: "poster red" }, { h: "#2a57a8", n: "cobalt" }, { h: "#f0dd4e", n: "flat yellow" }, { h: "#f7f2e6", n: "paper" }, { h: "#26221f", n: "outline black" }],
