    desc: "John James Audubon spent the 1810s–20s shooting, wiring and painting American birds life-size in active, often violent poses — an ornithological revolution against stiff specimen tables. The 435 plates were engraved chiefly by Robert Havell Jr. in London and issued in parts to subscribers between 1827 and 1838 as The Birds of America, printed on double-elephant folio paper roughly 97 by 65 centimetres so even herons and flamingos fit full-length. Each print was hand-coloured in watercolour over aquatint and line engraving, mixing ornithology with romantic landscape drama. A cheaper lithographed octavo edition in the 1840s democratized the images. Roughly 120 complete folio sets survive; one sold at Sotheby's for about eleven million dollars in 2010, and the plates remain touchstones for natural-history art and conservation iconography — the National Audubon Society takes its name from him.",
    col: [{ h: "#f4efe2", n: "ivory plate" }, { h: "#2a2723", n: "engraving ink" }, { h: "#b23a48", n: "carmine" }, { h: "#5f7d6b", n: "verdigris" }, { h: "#c69a4e", n: "ochre" }],
