    n: "Asemic writing", a: ["asemic script", "wordless calligraphy", "unreadable writing"],
    cat: "Drawing & Line Work", c: "writing-shaped marks that carry no readable content", e: "historical", o: "Roots in symbolist experiments, Surrealist automatic writing and Henri Michaux's ink works; named as a field by contemporary poets and artists in the 2000s (Tim Gaze, Cecil Touchon, asemic.net)",
    g: "International network; strong Australian, US and European scenes", p: "precursors through the 20th century; consolidated 1997–present", era: "post-conceptual writing art",
    sum: "Calligraphy-like mark-making that mimics script while carrying no readable text, read instead as gesture and rhythm.",
    desc: "Asemic writing produces marks that look like script — lines, loops, pseudo-letters, full page logic — while deliberately signifying nothing readable. Its lineage runs through symbolist experiments and Surrealist automatic writing to Henri Michaux's ink rhythms, whose 1951 book Mouvements assembled hundreds of wordless invented signs. Postwar and contemporary practitioners such as Mira Schendel, with her 1960s monotype graphics, and poets Tim Gaze and Cecil Touchon consolidated a named field in the 2000s around asemic.net and Asemic Magazine. Viewers scan asemic pages the way they scan handwriting — for gesture, pressure and rhythm rather than content — so every reading is personal and unstable. The practice sits between writing, drawing and abstraction, thrives in artist books, mail art and online communities, and deliberately troubles the boundary between language and image.",
    col: [{ h: "#1c1a17", n: "ink black" }, { h: "#f5f0e4", n: "paper cream" }, { h: "#6d675c", n: "graphite grey" }, { h: "#a33b2e", n: "seal red" }, { h: "#7d8a8f", n: "wash grey-blue" }],
