    cat: "Drawing & Line Work", c: "surrealist collaborative drawing game", e: "historical", o: "Paris surrealists around André Breton, c. 1925; named for a sentence the game produced — 'the exquisite corpse will drink the new wine'",
    g: "France, then international classrooms and studios", p: "c. 1925–present", era: "surrealist movement and after",
    sum: "The surrealists' folded-paper game: each player draws a body section unseen, assembling marvelous hybrid figures collectively.",
    desc: "Exquisite corpse (cadavre exquis) began around 1925 among Paris surrealists as a writing game, named for a sentence it produced. Players fold a sheet so only the previous section's edge shows, then draw a head, torso or legs in ignorance of the rest; unfolded, the sheet reveals impossible composite bodies that no single author planned. André Breton's circle documented the drawings in La Révolution surréaliste, treating results as evidence of collective inspiration and chance — core surrealist values set against authored style. The rules migrated to collages, photographs and word games, and survived as a classroom and studio warm-up across the twentieth century. MoMA Learning uses it in education programs, and contemporary comics jams, corpse murals and online collaborative chains keep the folded edge alive.",
