    n: "Ensō zen circle", a: ["zen brush circle", "ensō calligraphy", "one-stroke circle"],
    cat: "Drawing & Line Work", c: "Zen Buddhist calligraphic circle", e: "historical", o: "Chan (Zen) Buddhist calligraphy in China, flourishing in Japan; associated with Edo-period masters Hakuin Ekaku and Sengai Gibon",
    g: "Japan principally; Zen institutions worldwide", p: "Edo period (1603–1868) onward; living practice", era: "Edo-period Zen culture to present",
    sum: "A Zen brush circle drawn in one breath — an open or closed ring whose ink character records the mind of the moment.",
    desc: "The ensō is a circle brushed in a single stroke, most closely associated with Japanese Zen calligraphy and transmitted from Chan Buddhist China. Monks and masters brush it during or after meditation as both discipline and statement: the circle may be closed and grounded or left open, its gap a deliberate emptiness. Ink load, speed and pressure are legible in the stroke — wet pools, dry-brush flicks and tapered entries read as the calligrapher's state of mind, which is why no two ensō are alike and why copies are considered dead. Edo-period masters Hakuin Ekaku and Sengai Gibon left celebrated examples, often hung in tokonoma alcoves of temples and tea rooms. The form entered global design vocabulary as a shorthand for mindfulness and wholeness, appearing in logos, tattoos and wellness branding — a circulation some practitioners greet with amusement.",
