    cat: "Drawing & Line Work", c: "technical legal line illustration", e: "historical", o: "Modern patent systems from the 18th century onward; rules standardized in offices such as the USPTO (Manual of Patent Examining Procedure)",
    g: "International patent offices; USPTO conventions most copied", p: "18th century–present; standardized from the late 19th century", era: "industrial-property bureaucracy",
    sum: "Standardized black-and-white technical line drawings that turn inventions into legal figures — an accidental design classic.",
    desc: "Patent drawings are the figure sheets attached to patent applications, governed by strict office rules: black India-ink line on white paper, no colour unless required, shading only when needed for understanding, reference numerals tied to leader lines, and figure labels in a fixed format. Samuel Morse's 1840 telegraph patent and the Szilard–Einstein refrigeration patent (1930) show the genre's range from contraption to diagram. Because drawings must teach the invention to an examiner without ornament, they developed a neutral, mathematical beauty — exploded views, ghosted interior lines, heroic cutaways — that modern viewers retroactively read as retro-futurist art. A whole draftsman trade grew around the rules, now largely replaced by CAD linework. Google Patents and museum print shops have made the sheets a popular decorative and teaching archive.",
    col: [{ h: "#f7f6f2", n: "paper white" }, { h: "#1a1916", n: "india ink" }, { h: "#8c8a84", n: "graphite grey" }, { h: "#a33b2e", n: "stamp red" }, { h: "#e8dcae", n: "aged yellow" }],
