    sum: "Charged solar particles funnel along magnetic field lines into curtains of green and red light over the polar night sky.",
    desc: "Aurorae shine when solar-wind electrons and ions are guided by Earth's magnetosphere into the upper atmosphere, exciting oxygen and nitrogen into glowing curtains and rays roughly between 100 and 300 kilometres altitude. Oxygen's green emission dominates, with red above it and nitrogen blues and purples lower down; the lights ripple in arcs, bands and corona bursts during geomagnetic storms driven by solar activity and its eleven-year cycle. Arctic peoples observed the lights for millennia, and physical explanation arrived only through the twentieth century, from Birkeland's experiments to confirmed particle precipitation. The aurora borealis has its southern mirror in the aurora australis. Iceland, Norway, Alaska and northern Canada run tourism seasons around it, and the Geophysical Institute in Fairbanks publishes the standard aurora forecast that hunters plan their nights by.",
