    sum: "Living chemistry made visible — fireflies, glowing squid and sea sparkle turning darkness into cold blue-green light.",
    desc: "Bioluminescence is light produced by living chemistry: luciferin oxidizing under luciferase enzymes, tuned by species into flashes, glows and startled clouds used to hunt, hide, lure and mate. Fireflies flash courtship codes over summer fields; the dinoflagellate Noctiluca scintillans, called sea sparkle, lights wake trails in warm bays; Waitomo's glowworm larvae dangle sticky light-lines in New Zealand caves; firefly squid mass along Toyama Bay each spring. The deep sea hosts most luminous life on Earth, from anglerfish lures to siphonophore chains, and rare milky seas of glowing bacteria have been visible from satellites. Mosquito Bay on Vieques, Puerto Rico, is celebrated as the brightest bioluminescent bay, drawing night paddlers through glowing wakes. The same chemistry powers modern biology's reporter genes and imaging probes, making the phenomenon both travel icon and laboratory tool.",
