    sum: "Sunlight shafted through cloud gaps and made visible by haze — the god rays that turn any sky into theatre.",
    desc: "Crepuscular rays appear when sunlight streams through breaks in clouds or terrain and is scattered into visible columns by airborne dust and droplets. The shafts are actually parallel, but perspective makes them fan from the sun's point, stretching across the sky and into the frames of Romantic painting, cathedral photography and ski films. When haze sits opposite the sun, the same geometry produces anticrepuscular rays converging at the antisolar point, a favourite of sky watchers. The name comes from twilight, when low sun angles make the beams most vivid, but they occur whenever light, gap and haze align. Art history's affair with the effect runs from Caspar David Friedrich to the wall-calendar sublime, and 3D engines shipped dedicated god-ray volumetric lighting from the 2000s onward, making the look a graphical signature of the Crysis era.",
    col: [{ h: "#f5d98c", n: "sun-beam gold" }, { h: "#cfd8e0", n: "haze grey-blue" }, { h: "#6f7f8c", n: "cloud slate" }, { h: "#b9c7b4", n: "hazy sage" }, { h: "#3c3527", n: "ray-shadow brown" }],
