    n: "Datamosh", a: ["P-frame smearing", "keyframe-smear art", "compression smear art"],
    cat: "Visual Effects & Phenomena", c: "video-compression corruption aesthetic", e: "internet_aesthetic", o: "Video-art experiments of the mid-2000s (Takeshi Murata); mainstreamed by Kanye West's Welcome to Heartbreak video (2009) and spread through YouTube and Vimeo glitch communities",
    g: "Internet-native; net-art and music-video scenes worldwide", p: "c. 2003–present", era: "post-digital glitch era",
    sum: "Compression corruption as art: deleted keyframes let motion bleed and melt across digital video in psychedelic smears.",
    desc: "Datamoshing exploits how video codecs work. MPEG-family compression stores full keyframes (I-frames) and then describes later frames only as changes (P-frames); remove the keyframes and the changes apply to nothing, so movement smears, blooms and ghosts across the frame in liquid blocks. Artists reach the effect by cutting I-frames in tools like Avidemux or by corrupting data streams deliberately. Takeshi Murata's Untitled (Silver) (2005) demonstrated the aesthetic on reprocessed footage, and Kanye West's Welcome to Heartbreak (directed by Nabil, 2009) pushed it into the mainstream as a deliberate contrast with glossy video. Crystal Castles' Plague video (2012) and countless net-art experiments followed, and the term became general slang for any compression-smear look. The style is a cornerstone of glitch art's celebration of digital material failure.",
    col: [{ h: "#241f3a", n: "deep block navy" }, { h: "#e84a7a", n: "smear magenta" }, { h: "#00e0d5", n: "ghost cyan" }, { h: "#f5f0e6", n: "hot white" }, { h: "#8b5cf6", n: "channel violet" }],
