    cat: "Visual Effects & Phenomena", c: "display-technology light effect", e: "historical", o: "Cathode-ray tube displays from 1930s television to 2000s monitors and arcades; phosphor formulations (P1 green, P3 amber, P4 white, P22 colour triads) define the look",
    g: "Global — offices, terminals, arcades, control rooms", p: "c. 1930s–2000s hardware era; emulated deliberately since", era: "retro-computing and broadcast era",
    sum: "The soft bloom of cathode-ray tubes — phosphor dots and persistence that gave early screens their green, amber and colour halos.",
    desc: "Cathode-ray tube displays paint images with electron beams exciting phosphor coatings, whose chemistry defines the look: P1 phosphor glows green on the classic terminal screen, P3 gives warm amber office monochrome, P4 white-blue marks early television, and colour CRTs use triads of red, green and blue phosphors. Phosphor persistence — the glow's decay time — slightly smears fast-moving text and images, and beam bloom wraps bright characters in halos, while curved glass and visible scan structure complete the signature. Long-exposure photography of arcade cabinets and terminals amplified the bloom into an aesthetic now emulated on purpose: shader packages like CRT-Royale and retro-console filters recreate phosphor glow, persistence and tube geometry for LCD and OLED screens. The look stands for the history of computing and a warmth that modern flat panels deliberately chase.",
